<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Russian Revolution of 1917</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian Revolution of 1917 brought down the Russian monarchy and led to the first communist state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> to abdicate and created a Provisional Government, which lost support as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> under Lenin seized power, and their victory in the civil war that followed led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-revolution.jpg" alt="Russian Revolution" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown; photo retake by George Shuklin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B5_(1917).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Russian Revolution of 1905 was a forerunner of 1917. Bloody Sunday triggered nationwide protests and soldier mutinies, and a council of workers, the St. Petersburg Soviet, was created. The revolution was crushed, but it led to the creation of a Duma, which later formed the Provisional Government. Nicholas II was a deeply conservative ruler who believed that his power was granted by Divine Right and, even after 1905 moved him to decree limited civil rights and democratic representation, worked to limit them.</p><p>The 1914 Battle of Tannenberg was a disaster for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with over 30,000 troops killed or wounded and 90,000 captured. In autumn 1915 Nicholas took direct command of the army and left his wife Alexandra in charge of the government. Reports of corruption and incompetence spread, and the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigori-rasputin">Grigori Rasputin</a> was widely resented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 7 March [O.S. 22 February] 1917 a strike closed the Putilov plant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>'s largest. On International Women's Day women marched to nearby factories and brought out over 50,000 workers, and by 10 March virtually every industrial enterprise in the city was shut. Nicholas abdicated on 15 March [O.S. 2 March], and a provisional government led by the Duma took over. The Petrograd Soviet claimed to represent workers and soldiers, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had two rival centres of power: the Provisional Government held authority in military and international affairs, the Soviets in domestic matters.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> won support with a programme of peace, land and bread, while the Provisional Government continued the war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> began on 7 November [O.S. 25 October], an armed insurrection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> organised by the Revolutionary Military Committee under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>; its initial assault occurred largely without casualties. The Bolsheviks proclaimed the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in March 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many groups did not recognise the Bolshevik government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> fell into a civil war between the Reds and an array of enemies including nationalists, other socialists, anarchists, monarchists and liberals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and his family at Yekaterinburg on 16 July 1918. Historians consider the revolutionary period to have ended in 1922 with the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Army</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was formed from the Soviet republics. Historians divide into Soviet-Marxist, Western totalitarian and revisionist schools; the totalitarian school describes a coup by a minority, the revisionists stress the revolution's popular character.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russian Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Russian Revolution</b> the Democratic Republic of Georgia declared independence on 26 May 1918 under the Menshevik Noe Zhordania, one of the first states in Europe to give women the vote; the Red Army entered Tbilisi on 25 February 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Russian Revolution of 1917</b> the Finns moved towards separation, and on 6 December 1917 parliament approved the declaration of independence presented by the Senate under Pehr Evind Svinhufvud.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet Union grew out of the <b>Russian Revolution of 1917</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Russian Revolution of 1917</b> several factions sought an independent Ukrainian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A rebellion of sailors at Wilhelmshaven on 29 October became the Kiel mutiny on 3 November, and workers' and soldiers' councils modelled on the soviets of the <b>Russian Revolution</b> spread across Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some on the left were drawn to the experiments that followed the <b>Russian Revolution</b>, such as constructivism, though Gropius did not share their views.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Background</a></li></ul></section>
